 "drivingQuestion": "Is one liable for a false oath about another person's past act?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "dispute stated; explained in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.25a.17",
   "daf": "25a",
   "sentenceId": 16,
   "he": "״שֶׁאֶזְרוֹק צְרוֹר לַיָּם״ וְ״שֶׁלֹּא אֶזְרוֹק״. אִיתְּמַר: ״שְׁבוּעָה שֶׁזָּרַק פְּלוֹנִי צְרוֹר לַיָּם״ וְ״שֶׁלֹּא זָרַק״, רַב אָמַר חַיָּיב, וּשְׁמוּאֵל אָמַר פָּטוּר.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that an example of an oath on an utterance is where one took an oath, saying: «I will throw a stone into the sea, or: I will not throw» it. It «was stated:» With regard to one who says: On my «oath so-and-so threw a stone into the sea, or:» On my «oath he did not throw» it, «Rav says:» If it was later discovered that what he said was false, he is «liable» to bring an offering for his oath. «And Shmuel says:» He is «exempt.»"
  }
 ],
